Dagu bounded read/mmap observation probe
========================================

`probe.c` runs exactly one bounded file operation per invocation against a
small fixture whose byte at file offset N is `N mod 256`:

    probe read|mmap FILE OFFSET LENGTH [--dontneed] [--sequential|--random]

The optional access-pattern flags are mutually exclusive and may appear in
either order relative to `--dontneed`. `--sequential` and `--random` select
`POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL`/`POSIX_FADV_RANDOM` for read mode, and
`MADV_SEQUENTIAL`/`MADV_RANDOM` for mmap mode. Read mode applies
`posix_fadvise` to the requested range before `pread`. Mmap mode creates the
requested VMA, applies `madvise` to it, then touches the selected byte. Output
records `hint`, `hint_api`, and `hint_status`; `not-applied` means cache
preparation prevented the access from running. A successful advisory call
records its application, not a guaranteed policy effect.

`read` issues one `pread` for the requested offset and length. It retries only
when the syscall returns `EINTR`; a regular-file short result before EOF is a
failure. A request may end at or cross EOF, and the reported return count is
the number of available bytes. A request beginning at EOF returns zero.

`mmap` maps the requested extent once and touches only its first byte. The
reported `mmap_offset` is the page-aligned offset passed to `mmap`; its length
includes the alignment delta. `touch_offset` is the logical file offset that
was read. The requested range must be fully within the file so the touch
cannot raise `SIGBUS`. Both modes verify the observed data against the fixture
pattern. The file must be regular and no larger than 16 MiB; requests are
bounded to a 16 MiB extent and a 1 MiB length.

Each successful operation reports the requested offset/length, the actual
`pread` or `mmap` arguments, data validation, and resident-page counts before
and after for both the whole fixture (`mincore_file_*`) and the accessible
requested range (`mincore_request_*`). The whole-file counts include pages
outside the request, which can reveal residency growth beyond the touched
range. `mincore` counts are auxiliary cache residency observations: they do
not identify demand versus speculative reads, prove storage I/O, or attribute
residency to readahead. The probe's `mmap` workload uses `MAP_PRIVATE`;
mincore uses separate read-only `MAP_SHARED` mappings.

`elapsed_ns` uses `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`. In read mode it brackets only the
`pread` syscall attempt(s), including an `EINTR` retry. In mmap mode it
brackets only the single-byte load that can fault the page. It excludes file
open, mapping setup, hint calls, data checking, mincore, and cache preparation.
It is a diagnostic sample, not a performance claim.

The optional `--dontneed` calls `posix_fadvise(POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED)` for the
entire fixture (`offset=0`, `length=0`, through EOF). Use it only with a
fixture you created for this test. The probe never writes file contents,
drops global caches, or changes system settings. After the advice, it checks
mincore for the whole fixture and requested range before running the
operation. It continues with `cold=confirmed` only when the whole fixture has
at least one page and both counts report zero resident pages. Otherwise it
prints `operation=skipped`, reports `cold=not-confirmed` (or `cold=unknown`
if the advice failed), marks the post-operation counts `not-run`, and exits
nonzero. `cold=confirmed` means mincore saw
no resident page in that fixture at the pre-operation check; it is not proof
that the next read will issue storage I/O.

Build a static AArch64 binary and run the behavior test in the Linux
container:

    aarch64-linux-gnu-gcc -O2 -Wall -Wextra -Werror -static \
        -o /tmp/dagu-readahead-probe probe.c
    python3 test-probe.py /tmp/dagu-readahead-probe

The test creates private temporary fixtures and exercises bounded `pread`,
mmap alignment and one-byte validation, partial/exact EOF, invalid mmap EOF,
empty fixtures, corrupt bytes, whole-file and request-range mincore output,
both access hints in both modes, conflicting-hint rejection, hint plus cache
preparation, and the cache-preparation rule including EOF. A successful host
test validates this userspace contract only; it does not replace target
kernel tracing or establish demand-versus-speculative I/O.

Example invocations on a known fixture:

    python3 - <<'PY'
    from pathlib import Path
    Path('/tmp/dagu-ra-fixture').write_bytes(bytes(range(256)) * 48)
    PY
    /tmp/dagu-readahead-probe read /tmp/dagu-ra-fixture 4096 4096
    /tmp/dagu-readahead-probe mmap /tmp/dagu-ra-fixture 4103 17
    /tmp/dagu-readahead-probe read /tmp/dagu-ra-fixture 4096 4096 \
        --dontneed --sequential
    /tmp/dagu-readahead-probe mmap /tmp/dagu-ra-fixture 4103 17 --random

The fixture must contain the expected byte pattern. The `--dontneed` form is
explicitly opt-in and should be limited to a dedicated fixture file.

For the limited-readahead experiment, mmap also accepts `--fd-random` once,
independently of the VMA `--sequential`/`--random` hint. It applies
POSIX_FADV_RANDOM to this open fd before mmap, reports `fd_random=applied`,
and combines with `--sequential` to exercise FMODE_RANDOM + VM_SEQ_READ.
Read mode rejects this option. A cold-preparation skip occurs before applying
it. This is a workload trigger, not evidence of a limit; inspect the actual
kernel decision event's demand_size=1, force=1 and raw/final tuples.
